severe on–off phenomena and peak dyskinesias. Laboratory
tests, including serum ceruloplasmin and copper, urinary ol-
igosaccharides, sulfatides, and leucocyte lysosomal
enzymes, were normal. FLAIR and T2-weighted brain MRI,
performed at 1.5 Tesla, revealed high intensity of the corti-
cospinal tracts extending from internal capsules to cerebral
peduncles. [
123I]FP-CIT single photon emission computed
tomography (SPECT) revealed symmetric reduced binding
in both striata, which was more severe in the putamen bilat-
erally (Fig. 2A). A [
123I]iodobenzamide-SPECT study
showed reduced striatal bi nding, more pronounced in the
right caudate and right putamen (Fig. 2C).
Because these ﬁndings were consistent with a diagnosis of
early-onset parkinsonism with autosomal recessive inheri-
tance, we carried out a genetic analysis of PRKN. The patient
was found to be a compound heterozygote for a missense
mutation C820T (T240M) in exon 6 and a large deletion
including exons 2, 3, and 4.
Both onset and progression were atypical in our patient.
The onset was characterized by palpebral ptosis, ataxic-
spastic gait, followed 4 years later by the occurrence of an
extrapyramidal syndrome with rigidity and dystonia, re-
