two biological activities: autoimmunity/inﬂammation and
neurodegeneration.6 Our patient with PPMS clearly combines
inﬂammatory and neurodegenerative features that could
explain the majority of the clinical manifestations and labora-
tory ﬁndings. In addition, we report positive ABGA in MS
for the ﬁrst time.
These autoantibodies bind to human basal ganglia, as well
recombinant antigens, with the highest binding speciﬁcity for
caudate and putamenal neurons. Their presence has been
reported to be associated with a wide spectrum of movement
(hyper- and hypokinetic) and neuropsychiatric disorders
including encephalitis lethargica, Sydenham’s chorea, and
Tourette’s syndrome.
7 The clinical phenotype of our patient
extends the list of ABGA-related disorders. However, a
casual rather than causal relationship cannot be completely
excluded. Although our patient did not appear to respond to
intravenous steroids, immunomodulatory therapies (steroids,
IVIG, and plasma exchange) have been reported to beneﬁt
patients with ABGA.
7 However, experience with other
ABGA-related disorders underscores the importance of early
immunotherapy initiation. 7 Late intervention as in our case,
predict only limited outcomes. Further research of ABGA is
warranted because presence of such antibody may be the sub-
strate for a variety of potentially treatable secondary move-
ment disorders.
