The symptoms remained unchanged during treatment with
baclofen (up to 40 mg/day, for 1 month) or diazepam (30
mg/day for 20 days). After 3 months, intravenous immuno-
globulin was started and repeated for 1 year (initial dose:
0.4 g/kg/day for ﬁve consecutive days, followed by monthly
doses of 0.4 g/kg administered for 1 day). During this pe-
riod, the patient showed signiﬁcant improvement of rigidity
and gait performance with mild improvement in dysarthria
(video 2: after 1 year of follow-up and video 3: after 3
years of follow-up).
The case reported here describ es a patient with a distinct
clinical presentation of SPS, namely painful stiffness in
three limbs and dysarthria, with no features such as sphinc-
ter disturbances, truncal rigid ity, exaggerated lumbar lordo-
sis, or CMUA.</seg> In 1956, Moersch and Woltman
2 ﬁrst
described 14 patients with progressive ﬂuctuating muscular
rigidity and spasms, most prom inent in axial and proximal
limb muscles, naming the disorder as stiff-man syndrome,
which today is referred to as SPS since this rare condition
actually has a predilection for women. Clinical variants
were later reported by Brown et al.
3 who described four
patients with a chronic progre ssive disorder characterized
by painful involuntary spasms, stiffness, and CMUA con-
ﬁned to the lower limbs. Another series of 23 patients with
SPS described a subgroup of 13 who had SLS with rigidity,
painful spasm, and abnormal postures of the distal limb,
usually the leg.
1 An autoimmune pathogenesis of SPS is
suggested by the observation of positive anti-GAD, the
clinical response to immune modulation, the association
with other autoimmune diseases including type 1 diabetes
mellitus, and the high incidence of organ-speciﬁc autoanti-
bodies.
4,5
<seg id="s0004" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">We designated the case presented here as STLS (asymmet-
rical rigidity in three limbs, associated with dysarthria, with-
out continuous motor unit activity), successfully treated with
intravenous immunoglobulin.
